{
  "version": 236,
  "released": "2026-08-27",
  "changelog": "v236: the owner's birth is the owner's, and a correction can be corrected (lifehug#255, contract docs/pr-specs/eras-o-e0-immediate-defects.md O-E0b/O-E0d, the Eras controlling design §3.1 and §8 step 1, ADR 0030). Two of E0's four immediate defects; O-E0a (honest probe anchors) and O-E0c (period_bound has no wrong writer) land beside it.\n\nWHY THE BIRTH ONE. `landmark_projection.entry_subject_mention` falls back to the DOMAIN WORD for an entry that names no subject, and a birth entry never names one — its ladder is three date grains. So the founder's own birthday was filed as a claim about somebody called \"birth\". That read as a person, and it worked only for as long as there was exactly one birth in the vault. The day a CHILD's birth was filed, the fold's seeding matched two births under the owner's key, matched none, fell through to \"any birth at all\", found two of those as well, and seeded NO birth anchor. Every age claim the person had ever made lost its arithmetic in one step, and a `missing_anchor birth_date self` work item opened against a vault that knew the birthday perfectly well. That is the executed probe in the design folder, reproduced here as T-BO-01.\n\nTHE BIRTH DOMAIN NOW SAYS `self`. `entry_subject_mention` mints `self` for `birth` unconditionally — it is the ONE domain whose subject is the person, and every other domain is byte-identical (T-E0-05 parametrises the whole question set to prove it). LEGACY RECEIPTS ARE READ, NOT REWRITTEN AND NOT RE-HARVESTED: `identity_resolution.is_owner_birth_domain_word` fires on the word AND the birth event kind together, and `owner_birth_domain_resolution` maps it to the owner through a `ResolutionRecord` carrying the new named reason `owner_birth_domain_word` — provenance-bearing, visible, and reversible through the same `unresolve` as a roster match or an owner verdict. The receipt still says what it said. A legacy receipt and a new `self` receipt for the same birth therefore group into ONE node and reconcile through `chronology.reconcile`, so an extraction-rule change can never mint two contradictory owner births (T-BO-01b, both orderings).\n\nAND THE FALLBACK IS DELETED. \"If that is not exactly one owner birth, take whatever birth exists\" was wrong in BOTH directions — with a child's birth filed it matched two and picked nothing, and with none of the owner's filed it would silently promote somebody else's birthday to the owner's age anchor. Seeding is now the owner's birth under either spelling, or nothing. `_resolve_subjects` keeps the rule's cache and the roster resolver's cache SEPARATE, because both are keyed by mention and a vault holding a birth claim and some other claim that happens to say \"birth\" would otherwise answer whichever arrived first — an order-dependent identity, which is the one thing this fold promises never to have.\n\nWHY THE CORRECTION ONE. A `source_correction` is immutable, which is the point: what was once believed survives what is believed now. But `corrections_for` returned EVERY correction targeting a source, so a correction could only ever be piled on, never corrected — and the reader's only tie-break was filename order, which is capture time wearing a disguise. The founder repair (§8) needs to retire one specific 2026-08-25 correction, and there was no verb for it.\n\n`correct --supersedes <source_id | path>` IS THAT VERB. The predecessor is not edited and not deleted; the NEW correction names it in `supersedes`/`supersedes_path`, and the readers stop reading the old one. Validated BEFORE anything is written, with three typed refusals — `supersedes_missing`, `supersedes_not_a_correction`, and the dangerous one, `supersedes_target_mismatch`, which would otherwise retire a correction of somebody else's story invisibly. A refusal leaves the vault exactly as it was.\n\nONE DEFINITION, MANY HOSTS (ADR 0021). `source_integrity.active_correction_leaves` is the graph; `active_corrections_for` is the disk reader over it; `classify_story.corrections_for` and the compiler's correction injection (`wiki_compile.active_corrections`) BIND to those rather than globbing the directory themselves, and an AST guard fails the build on a second module that does (T-E0-12). A correction is active unless SOME correction names it — deliberately not \"some ACTIVE one\", because in a chain A ← B ← C only C is active and any other reading would let a superseded text come back the moment the correction that replaced it was itself replaced. Two shapes are refused LOUDLY rather than absorbed, because both would silently RESTORE a text somebody said was wrong: an edge pointing outside the target's own set (`supersedes_outside_target_set`) and a cycle (`supersedes_cycle`, which the leaf rule alone would render as an empty active set — every correction gone, and no error to say so). The stale mark on the target is INHERITED, not re-implemented: `create_linked_source` already calls `classify_story.mark_stale`, and the test asserts it fired rather than assuming it.\n\nFOUND ON THE WAY: a linked source's filename identity is `(type, target, payload)`, so filing a superseding correction whose TEXT matched its predecessor would have landed on the existing file, skipped the write, and reported success while the supersession edge went nowhere. `supersedes` now joins that identity when present — and only when present, so every stem minted before this version is byte-identical.\n\nTESTS: `tests/test_eras_e0.py`, twenty cases, every negative one seen failing against unmodified v234 first (21 failed / 5 passed before; 20 passed after). WHAT YOU'LL NOTICE: your age statements keep dating themselves after your first child's birthday is filed — and when you correct a correction, the version you took back stops appearing in anything the system derives.",
